#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065125} :activity/id #uuid "00e08faa-77cf-50f3-bce1-d22a42a5f138" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048441 :topic/id #uuid "9faf7c3f-8d4f-5e48-97ed-6c10e2c5383a" :topic/math-academy-id 291} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065447 :tutorial/content "Consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x)= 4x^2+x, \\qquad x \\gt 0\n$$\n\nThe domain restriction $x > 0$ means that $f$ is invertible on its entire domain. \n\nThe point $P$ with coordinates $(2,18)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x).$ This means that the point $P'$ with coordinates $(18,2)$ lies on the curve of the inverse function $y=f^{-1}(x),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e16e532743be547c766e5a6a47bae8933a8dedb2b6d0fa0d60d47cc0b37ba04.png)\n\nHow do we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at the point $P'?$\n\nFor this, we will use the fact that the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at $P'$ is the *reciprocal* of the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $P.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{{P}^{′}}=\\frac{1}{({\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{P})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at $P$ is straightforward. First, we differentiate $y=f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 8x + 1,\n$$\n\nand then plugging in $x=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_P = 8(2)+1 = 17.\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_{P'}= \\dfrac{1}{17}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this process using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}\n$$\n\nTo help remember this formula, note that it's okay to swap the derivative symbol (the prime) with the inverse symbol (the exponent of $-1$) as long as we take the reciprocal afterward." :tutorial/id #uuid "a1f70bcd-9b56-5fdd-ba18-58b625c05af0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9132 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1da740a0-e78c-558d-b559-528c35ca81d7" :step/math-academy-id 187680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064251 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063999 :question/id #uuid "d113528f-a3ff-5f28-b071-bcf355a36b45" :question/math-academy-id "e-14720"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049352}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487398}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "16c7f862-0b61-56ca-b731-b881c09a79a7" :step/math-academy-id 187681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065127}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063998 :question/id #uuid "121d5f8c-3b38-5c75-aec1-646dc7d2f2f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14719"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505744}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"} :step/id #uuid "006b4fae-82f7-53a6-a492-7df130468acf" :step/math-academy-id 187684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065128}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065128 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065448 :tutorial/content "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and $f'(x)$ at some points. \n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $12$ |\n\nLet's use this data to find $(f^{-1})'(1).$ \n\nRecall that the formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(1) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(2)=1$, hence $f^{-1}(1)=2$, and also $f'(2)=12$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use the rule for calculating derivatives of inverse functions in conjunction with other rules of differentiation. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3e542fea-8d0a-5c0c-ba69-3ec1685ecf92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9136 :tutorial/title "Calculating Derivatives Using Tables"} :step/id #uuid "2950a7d5-38ee-59bf-b277-9aeb378c6c62" :step/math-academy-id 187687 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065129}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064000 :question/id #uuid "9e531872-846f-5e75-97c0-dd2db3515558" :question/math-academy-id "e-14721"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050961}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505765}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"} :step/id #uuid "5c3fc6b3-0d2e-50bb-98ce-b5e82b6f6a33" :step/math-academy-id 187688 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065130}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065130 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064001 :question/id #uuid "d38ac709-38e8-51a1-8e08-14913c77639c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14722"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050649}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505782}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"} :step/id #uuid "7584e271-65ff-5831-8847-5f5e3a737754" :step/math-academy-id 187691}] :activity/title "Differentiating an Inverse Function at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064396}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409839} :activity/id #uuid "f566dc84-1e6c-5c3c-9fcc-e05918f8418d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048441 :topic/id #uuid "9faf7c3f-8d4f-5e48-97ed-6c10e2c5383a" :topic/math-academy-id 291} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f3f95aa-4ff5-5778-912a-4e72354aee90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409824} {:answer/id #uuid "58770571-0d16-5811-81f1-369401a6b261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409825} {:answer/id #uuid "59d1a2f1-8dc8-5d29-a302-08e96496fd6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409826} {:answer/id #uuid "bb476700-47a3-54b6-8384-b9eab15af2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409829} {:answer/id #uuid "ff95022d-0315-5d9e-babd-36b52f088340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409826} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd53fe12-9eba-5b4c-ab0e-a56e97b0b5d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "14d699f1-906b-5c36-8ad3-387a50daa57f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16098" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $-1$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(0)=2$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "176bf3b7-7f3b-537d-967f-47062e4a2221" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01506915-9279-5845-be61-c4c35f7caa00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244319} {:answer/id #uuid "13eba5b3-5ee1-54a2-a165-599feede7427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244494} {:answer/id #uuid "1e2f0aad-80c7-52df-b8b1-92ab89cc1a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244578} {:answer/id #uuid "74346772-ee82-5598-92fc-961da1e54b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245348} {:answer/id #uuid "933b4cc2-b259-5d0b-aed0-2acad610e648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245597}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244578} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c107e1-441a-5e9e-a64c-c9d6f455a387" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "34f629c1-9fcd-59d6-b73f-996bb1a66a78" :question/math-academy-id "q-110347" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-3+5{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ -3+5{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ 5{e}^{x+1} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-3+5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a496245c-b30d-521d-bb6c-1805665cc858"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecb3b6c-5f9d-519e-ac19-a3e22b342ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409823} {:answer/id #uuid "614852f7-d26e-56cc-93b6-f07f9c417f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409827} {:answer/id #uuid "8f042161-1e15-525d-8dc1-31568976162b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409828} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7bca2f-0e02-5b17-90a9-f17776a41ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409830} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1bca86-26ee-5bcf-a659-0ec759203aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409823} :answer-field/id #uuid "65142116-0d83-53ff-8a22-53c3c70d6112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9c5ee55a-541c-5782-b80c-3f5d6eae3256" :question/math-academy-id "q-15643" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=12-6x$ and the point $P(3,-6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(-6,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(3,-6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =12-6x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-6\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(3,-6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-6,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-6)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0da1a86-46b3-5c10-bca9-de55fa913cde" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409837}}] :activity/title "Differentiating an Inverse Function at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409822}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487454} :activity/id #uuid "53942e05-26a9-5828-9055-a80f3d69fad5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048441 :topic/id #uuid "9faf7c3f-8d4f-5e48-97ed-6c10e2c5383a" :topic/math-academy-id 291} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487413 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b25b7d1-2205-5d45-9eb4-1153c39f2254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486678} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf1ee9d-fb31-555f-8234-c02cee50db01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486946} {:answer/id #uuid "cd994e00-b7e4-5872-9698-8e0bb1e9c264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487046} {:answer/id #uuid "f42704d7-ba6a-5ac9-b641-e73d8c7eb55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487116} {:answer/id #uuid "f70d98a5-70c4-581c-95be-04705131018f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487046} :answer-field/id #uuid "401d966c-98a8-5ffc-958c-1d19edfa6b7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2576954f-b0ee-571c-85ae-5533e6d22543" :question/math-academy-id "q-75836" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $5$ | $4$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=8$ and ${f}^{′}(8)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "29ee00b7-02cb-5105-a7be-7bc30258a733" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487425}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487421 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2be8b47e-8161-5ac6-af15-f112a84dd1b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315375} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdde61d2-0b02-5fd6-a68f-661c818e9a55" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53ed59d8-b22e-538d-9cc1-0f9210effe07" :question/math-academy-id "q-342596" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "410fa10e-cbe7-5086-9bf0-1df3c0ba90fb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43858207-8831-5183-b09f-a3da1530cacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486740} {:answer/id #uuid "59d574d6-2cda-524f-ba83-6266f545c2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486791} {:answer/id #uuid "64c98e51-6b85-5d4c-ac0f-82782d6de80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486815} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e519cc-a7bd-5d74-8524-f56366c5b993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486975} {:answer/id #uuid "c27521f8-15fb-507d-92a2-e35438e66960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486975} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c2d58ce-95e5-572a-9ac2-e040031c4dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "10dcb0f4-5f0f-5b93-a598-6c0cd2ed4435" :question/math-academy-id "q-76105" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ For $x\\ne \\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}\\cdot (2x-1) & =2\\cdot (2x-1) \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{\\cancel{(2x-1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(2x-1)} & =4x-2 \\\\ 3x+1 & =4x-2 \\\\ 1+2 & =4x-3x \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{5}{(2x-1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-\\frac{5}{{(2\\cdot 3-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{{(5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "53aab599-8ea7-5095-9e1f-104bd68f1330" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487430}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9f522c8-2e41-549d-a3cd-753ef997b9f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487000}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487000} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd876f7-79e5-597c-ac7b-34096cd74827" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f03606b0-4d82-587c-8251-04c53399de85" :question/math-academy-id "q-342572" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1735679cbf165e26ac22f1fd3076d450231ea8c20c1905ec74f008bc89ec33a.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(2,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $-1$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "660230ab-1276-560d-bdd5-765d8d1b7e46" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487421}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a9914a1-bd63-5503-bb36-c2533c2c5218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063355} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc67312-c83d-5cab-b917-ee2161d29129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063356} {:answer/id #uuid "c283d4fd-1d67-5c92-a428-3fc3d98830e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063357} {:answer/id #uuid "cad706a5-f7c0-5286-9b96-bf0ba4a525c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063358} {:answer/id #uuid "de134615-356c-50aa-8b5e-53d741c02ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063356} :answer-field/id #uuid "5881a3d1-5740-518f-bfdd-3ac99c905e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59090c66-6332-5a45-a658-fc8034750491" :question/math-academy-id "q-5028" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=4x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P(0,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f5586187-a46c-585d-9b8d-9f88bb39ba76" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487413}}] :activity/title "Differentiating an Inverse Function at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486589}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505883} :activity/id #uuid "895899f5-e0e0-50f3-bd94-fa13c6acacef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048441 :topic/id #uuid "9faf7c3f-8d4f-5e48-97ed-6c10e2c5383a" :topic/math-academy-id 291} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505862 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d86fb7c-04a2-5054-88db-d87318f91df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245716} {:answer/id #uuid "a7efd507-c58e-56ec-8841-05d7624ba7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245816} {:answer/id #uuid "b538f1e2-5740-5e86-9585-792990790c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245930} {:answer/id #uuid "cf18104f-33a0-52e9-88d9-69f208eb80ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246163} {:answer/id #uuid "f4626bee-994c-50c4-b9f0-fb3dfa085a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246468} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b79bcdf-534b-5594-b18b-23bc8c71894d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "697e17f6-464a-5495-90d8-1dc0fdda0186" :question/math-academy-id "q-110338" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+10}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ x+10 & =4x+4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=2.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+10}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{9}{{(2+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{9} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "455e56d5-f225-535e-825f-94cbf70b48fc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd15925-6269-57a7-be2d-c5bb4a253804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505158} :answer-field/id #uuid "3719cd77-cf8e-5efe-870f-d9b67a741f79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1fb55561-256d-590b-a263-00ef7bf01b04" :question/math-academy-id "q-342578" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=8x-{x}^{4}$ and the point $P(1,7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(7,1)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =8x-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =8-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(7,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "975b5dbf-d467-5d52-8a17-1f76882fba02" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de97e9b-167d-58b8-b3d5-94d7072f96fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244438} {:answer/id #uuid "411a9d60-45d9-5255-af7c-f54d7ac9a709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244886} {:answer/id #uuid "7480aff9-21a8-5cfa-9c98-bc986f215b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245351} {:answer/id #uuid "a3928f90-c565-5339-83c1-14d12d515196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{44}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245773} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d9aa97-30ab-5be0-9392-8e2eebc6259c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245351} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f27b98d-efdc-5fef-8552-5c0790435d8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "32e90e8e-86b1-556f-b1e0-fc9bc969a8b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-110284" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)=\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(3)=-3,$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3) & =\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{f}^{′}(-2)}+(-3)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-5}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a0294a61-633a-557c-873e-f9769cf7d36d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505862}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505765 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff4aa7f4-8cb5-5cc7-8654-d178bd47988c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505603} :answer-field/id #uuid "be729943-ac78-5a58-a4af-33ce85ae0d28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "513e391c-326d-528e-9c7c-38c97cf324b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-342598" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4)={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=2,$ $g(4)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & ={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{(-1)}{5} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "acef38f7-9530-5971-b272-29c746a33a1a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505891}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "521f1d1c-c4a9-54ce-be0a-26a5043158cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505268} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dc0e4ad-a39d-502a-8eb5-af62880a7035" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70bc61eb-58b7-5349-9bef-f900f312799d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342615" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ x & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d3f19cde-6964-5e90-b86b-fab6f1784841"}] :activity/title "Differentiating an Inverse Function at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505099}]}
